# Netlify redirect rules.
#
# One job so far: send the .netlify.app hostname to the custom domain.
#
# Netlify does not do this on its own. Star Stuff found on 2026-08-15 that all four
# of its addresses answered HTTP 200 with the same ETag — byte-identical documents
# at four URLs, which is a duplicate-content problem and an ambiguity about which
# address is the real one. This site had the same shape the moment queering.earth
# went live: both hostnames answered 200.
#
# Enabled 2026-09-07, the day the domain was attached. Before that it stayed
# commented out on purpose: redirecting the only working hostname to one that did
# not resolve would have been a site that was simply down.
#
# The project is `queering-earth` (id 4115815c-5811-4df6-8f6f-9598e85d2d72,
# Stimpunks team).

https://queering-earth.netlify.app/*  https://queering.earth/:splat  301!
http://queering-earth.netlify.app/*   https://queering.earth/:splat  301!

# ── The extension is not an address ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Added 2026-09-09 from the spec audit, which found the path-level version of the
# defect the hostname rules above already describe: every sheet answered 200 at
# BOTH /on-being-ill and /on-being-ill.html, byte-identical, same ETag. We wrote
# the reasoning down for the hostnames, fixed that layer on 7 September, and did
# not think to look one layer down.
#
# The house rule is that addresses are extensionless. check-sitemap.mjs enforces
# it in the manifest, and every rel="canonical" points the right way — and none of
# that stops the edge answering the .html twin, because NO FILE-LEVEL GUARD CAN
# SEE WHAT THE EDGE ACTUALLY ANSWERS. tools/check-addresses.mjs is the guard that
# can, and it is why these rules are enumerated rather than globbed: a new sheet
# without a rule is a failure the checker reports by name.
#
# THE `!` IS LOAD-BEARING. Netlify shadows a redirect with a real file at the same
# path, and on-being-ill.html IS a real file — so an unforced rule would never
# fire. This is the same 301! the hostname rules use, for the same reason.
#
# Netlify already got the near misses on its own, which is how this hid so long:
# /on-being-ill/ (trailing slash) and /on-being-ill.HTML (wrong case) both 301
# here correctly. Only the exact-case extension had a file to hide behind. The
# site redirected the misspelled extension and served the correct one.

/changelog.html                   /changelog                    301!
/coming-to-terms.html             /coming-to-terms              301!
/design.html                      /design                       301!
/flower-codes.html                /flower-codes                 301!
/invention-of-normal.html         /invention-of-normal          301!
/monotropa-uniflora.html          /monotropa-uniflora           301!
/on-being-ill.html                /on-being-ill                 301!
/promises-like-pie-crust.html     /promises-like-pie-crust      301!
/search.html                      /search                       301!
/ledger.html                      /ledger                       301!
/what-is-settled.html             /what-is-settled              301!
/mission.html                     /mission                      301!
/privacy.html                     /privacy                      301!
/the-tempest.html                 /the-tempest                  301!
/wild-nights.html                 /wild-nights                  301!

# The plate is `/`, not `/index.html`. Netlify already 301s the bare /index.
/index.html                       /                             301!

# ── The error page has no address ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# 404.html is a real file, so /404 and /404.html both answered 200 with the error
# page — a SOFT 404, which is the fault the spec's error-pages item leads with, and
# one we introduced by fixing the page. check-addresses.mjs --live reported both.
#
# These are REWRITES and not redirects: a non-3xx status serves the target's content
# at the requested path with that status, so the reader gets the herbarium 404 and
# the crawler gets a 404 to match. Netlify resolves a rewrite once and does not
# re-run the table against the target, so the self-reference on the second rule
# does not loop — verified live rather than assumed.
/404                              /404.html                     404!
/404.html                         /404.html                     404!
